{
	title: 'Queue edits lock one message, not the session',
	spec: `Editing a queued message locks that message only, not the session. The turn keeps working while the user edits: tools run, steering and messages from other sessions still reach the model. Starting an edit never pauses, aborts or ends a turn.

Order invariant: Hal never changes the order of queued messages without explicit user intent. When the turn ends, the first queued message is delivered as usual if it is not being edited, even if a later one is. If the first queued message is the one being edited, the session waits; messages behind it wait too. The status reads 'waiting for queue edit'. Save or cancel delivers it at once, in its place. The same rule applies wherever a waiting human message would leave the queue: /queue next, a prompt that takes waiting messages along, and any future delivery mode that releases waiting messages at the next round instead of at turn end.

Race invariant: lock and dequeue are atomic with respect to each other. Exactly one wins. If delivery wins, acquisition refuses with the full reason ('already delivered') and the editor returns the changed text to the draft. If the lock wins, delivery skips or waits as above; the message is never delivered with text that differs from what the user saved. Host tests cover both orders: turn end, /queue next and prompt take-along each racing acquisition, plus save and cancel while the turn ends.

Lock lifetime: one client edits at a time. The window that started the edit owns the lock; every other window (web or terminal) shows the mirrored text as read-only, labeled 'Editing queued message in another window', and offers no Enter or edit action that the host would refuse. Other windows cannot start editing any queued message of that session until the lock ends. Save or cancel in the owning window ends it everywhere. When the owning connection disconnects (window closed at once, crash or network loss after the disconnect is detected), the host releases the lock; the message stays unchanged and queued, delivery continues in order, and another window may then edit it. No durable session pause results from editing; a host restart during an edit releases the lock the same way. Unsaved text survives in the shared draft as today.

Visible plumbing: waiting cards show which queued message is being edited; refusals and released locks show their reason where they happened.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Current state (src/host/queue-edits.ts): acquisition pauses the session (aborting a running round) and writes sessions/<id>/queue-edit.ason so a new host stays paused; holds are owned by one connection object. Disconnect releases the hold but leaves the session paused. Bun websockets detect dead peers via ping/idle timeout (about 2 minutes), a closed window at once. Web drafts mirror across browser tabs through localStorage, including queueEdit, but host ownership is per connection: a mirroring tab showed the edited text in a plain composer and Enter was refused ('a queued message is being edited'). Host is single-threaded, so acquire and dequeue are atomic if each checks and acts synchronously.",
		'User chose blocking over handover: other clients cannot edit while one edits. This removes cross-window ownership transfer and text-conflict checks. Host snapshots already carry queueHold (the held message id), so other clients can show the read-only state from it.',
		'Implemented: src/host/queue-edits.ts keeps an in-memory lock per session (owner connection, edit id, message); no pause, no sessions/<id>/queue-edit.ason (removed from the home too). prompts.next and prompts.queueNext stop at the locked message; prompts.deliver throws if asked to deliver it, the invariant behind every dequeue. Release (save, cancel, owner disconnect or close) runs a deferred /queue next, or prompts.next when the session is idle. A queued submit while queued messages wait goes behind them, even in an idle session. Only /clear, /rebase, /queue clear, rebase-apply, rewinds and other edits of the locked message are refused while locked.',
		"Window identity: a client's shared store (localStorage, terminal draft files) is not live-mirrored; another window can load an edit when it first reads the store. QueueEditing.window names the owner window (web: sessionStorage id, survives reload; terminal: HAL_TAB_FILE of the ./run loop, survives Ctrl-R). Other windows ignore it; on a snapshot without the lock, or on begin, they re-read the store and adopt an edit left active as canceled, so its text returns on the next edit or goes to the draft if the message left. Acquire with the same edit id from a new connection transfers the lock: the owner window reconnecting before the host notices the old socket is gone.",
		"Interpretation for the user to confirm: other windows keep a normal composer (new prompts are accepted now), with the notice 'Editing queued message in another window', no queued-message Edit buttons, Up doing nothing more, and the card header '(queued, being edited)'. They show the queued message's saved text, not the live unsaved text: the host never receives unsaved edit text. Open questions no longer refuse queue edits: the refusal existed only because acquisition paused."
	],
	needs: ['jsg'],
	foldInto: ['jsg']
}
